From: =?UTF-8?q?Samuel=20Carre=C3=B1o?= <soyminero11yt@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 18:49:49 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: g15: keep the T6031 GFX domain on through system
 sleep
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The T6031 GPU is powered by its PMGR domain, gfx, alone, and the G15
holds that domain from its admission on, with a runtime-PM reference it
gives back only after a start that failed before the firmware was
touched: once started, the firmware session lasts until reboot, and
nothing starts it again. In the noirq phase, though, genpd counts the
GPU, the only device in gfx, as suspended, whatever its runtime-PM
state, and powers gfx off under the running firmware
(genpd_finish_suspend(), genpd_sync_power_off()). genpd spares a device
in the wakeup path only in a domain with GENPD_FLAG_ACTIVE_WAKEUP, which
PMGR gives only to a domain it finds on when it probes it, and gfx is
off then.

Leave the GPU out of system sleep instead, as a syscore device, for as
long as the G15 holds gfx, as dwc3-apple keeps its core's domain on and
the ISP and the SEP those of their resident firmware: the PM core skips
the GPU in every phase, genpd never counts it as suspended, and gfx
stays on, under afr, which is always on. asahi_g15_release_power()
clears the flag with the hold. The G15 has no callbacks to skip, and
nothing changes while the system is awake: gfx is powered when it was
before, and not at all in a boot without the G15.

As before, the hold covers a bound GPU only: when the probe fails after
the firmware was touched, its cleanup detaches the GPU from gfx, and
genpd may then power gfx off. The admission now reads the ASC control
word before it takes the hold, so that no failure there leaves the
hold, and the flag, behind.

Only on T6031, whose configuration admits the J516C alone, and unless
g15_sleep_hold=0, which restores the old behaviour; the admission logs
which of the two applies. T6030 is not changed.

Marking gfx apple,always-on from the display gate, as it marks the
PMP's own power states, would keep it on in sleep too, but PMGR would
then power gfx up when it probes it, before the PMP has started, instead
of at the G15's admission (in a boot of 29-oct: PMGR turned its
always-on domains on at 1.62 s, the PMP was started at 2.17 s, the G15
admitted at 3.44 s), and keep it up in boots where the G15 never starts.

Nothing else is changed for sleep: the GPU mailbox's interrupts are
IRQF_NO_SUSPEND and neither the G15's poll work nor RTKit's is
freezable, so the firmware is still served through the sleep, each
message waking a CPU briefly without ending it; the G15's deadlines run
on CLOCK_MONOTONIC, which s2idle does not advance here (cpuidle-apple's
states have enter_s2idle, so the tick and timekeeping are frozen); and
the firmware is not told about the sleep.

Tested on a J516C with the G15 running, on top of local patches that
let it sleep and wake (among them the sleep work of #274 ported to the
J516C, with its RTC alarm): pm_test=platform, then three s2idle sleeps
of 30 s, each ended by the RTC alarm. gfx was never powered off (genpd
counted no new entry into its off state), the GPU mailbox raised 0 to 8
interrupts in each sleep without ending it, and the GPU ran compute work
after each one. That gfx goes off in the noirq phase without this is
read from genpd, not observed: before this change no system sleep went
past pm_test=devices with the G15 running.

